    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, my client has been stuck on their squat for like 3 weeks now. they can't seem to add any weight to the bar even though they're eating enough. how do deload weeks and changing up the volume actually help break a plateau like this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A common phenomenon in weightlifting. Deload weeks and changing up volume can help break a plateau by allowing the body to recover from overtraining, build in new adaptations, and increase blood flow back to the muscle tissue that requires repair. This can stimulate cellular growth, increase production of growth factors, and enhance neural drive to the muscles, making it easier to lift heavier weights in the long run. By reducing the intensity and volume of training, the body can rebalance its energy state, and the plateaus are broken.",
      "source": "target",
