    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Is it mostly about the physical muscle recovery or is the central nervous system part of it too? I've heard people say plateaus happen when the brain just stops sending the right signals even if the muscles feel fine.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Central Nervous System (CNS) plays a significant role in breaking plateaus. When the CNS adapts to the repetitive neural patterns and muscle contractions, it can actually decrease its neural drive, leading to a phenomenon called \"neural fatigue\". This doesn't necessarily mean the muscles are recovering, but rather the CNS is less \"receptive\" to the stimuli, making it harder to lift heavy weights. This neural adaptation can be overcome with changes to training, sleep, and recovery, rather than just physical muscle recovery.",
      "source": "target",
