    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so when you're talking about the CNS being \"less receptive,\" are there specific ways we can tell if it's actually CNS fatigue versus just regular muscle soreness? like are there certain signs to look for during a session that say \"hey, your brain is fried\" rather than just your legs being tired?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, CNS fatigue can manifest differently than regular muscle soreness. Signs to look for include slower lift times, reduced force production, decreased catch range, and an overall feeling of disjointedness or lack of coordination. You may also notice that the muscles feel symmetrical, whereas with regular muscle soreness, one side tends to be affected more than the other. Another giveaway might be the feeling of mental fogginess, decreased focus, or an overall sense of fatigue that worsens during the workout, whereas regular muscle soreness tends to be more localized.",
      "source": "target",
